Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage

player
• player_id: join key to identify each player  
• player_name: just informational  
• country_name: group players by country for country‑level averages  

player_match
• player_id: join to player; lets us know which matches a player appeared in  
• match_id: count distinct matches played by each player  

ball_by_ball
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no: join keys to batsman_scored  
• striker: the player who faced the ball (the run scorer)  

batsman_scored
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no: join keys to ball_by_ball  
• runs_scored: runs made by the striker on that delivery (ignore extras)  

Conditions that may be useful  
• Join ball_by_ball and batsman_scored on (match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no)  
• Aggregate runs_scored per striker to get total runs for each player  
• Count distinct match_id per player in player_match to get matches‑played  
• Filter out NULLs if any appear in the joining columns  



Exploratory SQL queries (each capped at 20 rows)

```sql
-- 1. List the different countries represented in the data
SELECT DISTINCT country_name
FROM player
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Sample of players with their countries
SELECT player_id,
       player_name,
       country_name
FROM player
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. How many matches has each player appeared in? (top 20)
SELECT player_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
FROM player_match
GROUP BY player_id
ORDER BY matches_played DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Peek at raw ball‑by‑ball records
SELECT match_id,
       over_id,
       ball_id,
       innings_no,
       striker,
       bowler
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join ball_by_ball with batsman_scored to see who scored what on a ball
SELECT b.match_id,
       b.over_id,
       b.ball_id,
       b.striker       AS batsman_id,
       bs.runs_scored
FROM   ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
  ON   b.match_id   = bs.match_id
 AND   b.over_id    = bs.over_id
 AND   b.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
 AND   b.innings_no = bs.innings_no
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Total career runs for each player (top 20)
SELECT b.striker   AS player_id,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS career_runs
FROM   ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
  ON   b.match_id   = bs.match_id
 AND   b.over_id    = bs.over_id
 AND   b.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
 AND   b.innings_no = bs.innings_no
GROUP  BY b.striker
ORDER  BY career_runs DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Number of matches played per player (repeat of #3 but kept for join)
SELECT player_id,
       COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
FROM   player_match
GROUP  BY player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Combine runs and matches to compute average runs per match for each player
SELECT r.player_id,
       r.career_runs,
       m.matches_played,
       1.0 * r.career_runs / m.matches_played AS avg_runs_per_match
FROM (
        SELECT b.striker AS player_id,
               SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS career_runs
        FROM   ball_by_ball AS b
        JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
          ON   b.match_id   = bs.match_id
         AND   b.over_id    = bs.over_id
         AND   b.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
         AND   b.innings_no = bs.innings_no
        GROUP  BY b.striker
     ) AS r
JOIN (
        SELECT player_id,
               COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
        FROM   player_match
        GROUP  BY player_id
     ) AS m
  ON r.player_id = m.player_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Average of those player averages for each country
SELECT p.country_name,
       AVG(sub.avg_runs_per_match) AS country_batting_average
FROM (
        -- reuse query #8 without LIMIT
        SELECT r.player_id,
               1.0 * r.career_runs / m.matches_played AS avg_runs_per_match
        FROM (
                SELECT b.striker AS player_id,
                       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS career_runs
                FROM   ball_by_ball AS b
                JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
                  ON   b.match_id   = bs.match_id
                 AND   b.over_id    = bs.over_id
                 AND   b.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
                 AND   b.innings_no = bs.innings_no
                GROUP  BY b.striker
             ) AS r
        JOIN (
                SELECT player_id,
                       COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
                FROM   player_match
                GROUP  BY player_id
             ) AS m
          ON r.player_id = m.player_id
     ) AS sub
JOIN player AS p
  ON p.player_id = sub.player_id
GROUP BY p.country_name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final answer: top 5 countries having the highest average of player batting averages
SELECT p.country_name                                                    AS country,
       ROUND(AVG(sub.avg_runs_per_match), 2)                             AS country_batting_average
FROM (
        SELECT r.player_id,
               1.0 * r.career_runs / m.matches_played AS avg_runs_per_match
        FROM (
                SELECT b.striker AS player_id,
                       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS career_runs
                FROM   ball_by_ball AS b
                JOIN   batsman_scored AS bs
                  ON   b.match_id   = bs.match_id
                 AND   b.over_id    = bs.over_id
                 AND   b.ball_id    = bs.ball_id
                 AND   b.innings_no = bs.innings_no
                GROUP  BY b.striker
             ) AS r
        JOIN (
                SELECT player_id,
                       COUNT(DISTINCT match_id) AS matches_played
                FROM   player_match
                GROUP  BY player_id
             ) AS m
          ON r.player_id = m.player_id
     ) AS sub
JOIN player AS p
  ON p.player_id = sub.player_id
GROUP BY p.country_name
ORDER BY country_batting_average DESC
LIMIT 5;
```
